      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "{\"id\":\"6e4b07aa-23dc-4146-a987-093fdbfa9da2\",\"title\":\"Ideation & Product Rationale — SFN-20: Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform\",\"content\":\"Backlog issue: [SFN-20 — Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-20/build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-markdown-authoring-platform>)\\n\\n## Decision summary\\n\\nBuild mdfn as a **Markdown-native, framework-neutral authoring platform** for the Superfunctions ecosystem.\\n\\nmdfn should not be a single editor component or a renderer-only package. It should provide:\\n\\n* A headless document, transaction, command, and extension layer\\n* A first-class Markdown parser, serializer, formatter, and diagnostics pipeline\\n* A vanilla DOM integration surface\\n* Equal adapters for React, Svelte, and Solid\\n* Composable editor UI built from UIFn\\n* Optional self-hosted collaboration, comments, suggestions, history, persistence, assets, and AI capabilities\\n* Explicit integrations for DocsFn, ContentFn, CmsFn, FileFn, SearchFn, AuthFn, and shared HTTP/database abstractions\\n\\nThe durable portable format is Markdown source. A structured runtime document model may power editing, but it must not become an undocumented replacement for the source contract.\\n\\n## Product opportunity\\n\\nSuperfunctions already has adjacent capabilities:\\n\\n* **DocsFn** compiles Markdown into renderable content blocks.\\n* **ContentFn** models portable structured content.\\n* **CmsFn** has authoring surfaces but no complete rich editing engine.\\n* **UIFn** provides a mature cross-framework UI architecture.\\n* **FileFn** can own durable assets and delivery.\\n* **SearchFn** can index normalized text, headings, and document chunks.\\n* **AuthFn**, shared HTTP packages, and shared database packages can support self-hosted multi-user services.\\n\\nThe missing layer is a shared authoring substrate. Today, products that need Markdown editing would otherwise need to independently solve selection, history, keyboard behavior, clipboard import, source mode, parser/serializer fidelity, UI behavior, extensions, framework bindings, persistence, collaboration, security, and migrations.\\n\\nmdfn turns those recurring costs into a reusable function and creates a single compatibility surface between editing, documentation, CMS, content, file, search, and application workflows.\\n\\n## Product thesis\\n\\nThe strongest product is one where:\\n\\n1. Markdown remains inspectable, portable, version-control-friendly, and durable.\\n2. Visual editing is powerful without making source fidelity accidental.\\n3. Framework adapters are bindings over one shared semantic engine.\\n4. UI chrome composes through UIFn but editing semantics do not depend on UIFn.\\n5. Self-hosted services are optional and use existing Superfunctions abstractions.\\n6. Unsupported syntax is preserved with diagnostics, never silently deleted.\\n7. Teams can begin with a headless editor and add collaboration or product integrations without changing the core document contract.\\n\\n## Primary users and jobs\\n\\n### Application developers\\n\\n* Embed a polished Markdown editor without accepting a framework lock-in.\\n* Add custom syntax, commands, node views, toolbar controls, and persistence.\\n* Use the same document behavior in multiple frontend stacks.\\n* Render the same source safely on servers, browsers, and documentation sites.\\n\\n### Documentation teams\\n\\n* Author CommonMark/GFM content visually or in source mode.\\n* Use frontmatter, code, tables, task lists, footnotes, math, callouts, diagrams, and custom directives.\\n* Keep source suitable for repositories and build pipelines.\\n* Preview exactly what will be published.\\n\\n### Product and CMS teams\\n\\n* Store Markdown as an explicit content type.\\n* Attach assets through FileFn without leaking temporary URLs into source.\\n* Add comments, suggestions, approvals, and history.\\n* Index content and headings through SearchFn.\\n\\n### Platform maintainers\\n\\n* Enforce one parser/serializer contract across products.\\n* Add extensions without forking core behavior.\\n* Migrate schemas and syntax versions safely.\\n* Test framework parity and security centrally.\\n\\n## Product principles\\n\\n### Markdown is the durable boundary\\n\\nThe persisted contract should always include Markdown. Runtime JSON may be cached or stored for performance, collaboration, or diagnostics, but it must be versioned and reproducible from the source contract.\\n\\n### Preservation is explicit\\n\\nThe product must distinguish three guarantees:\\n\\n1. **No-edit preservation:** importing and exporting without an edit returns byte-identical source.\\n2. **Semantic round trip:** supported syntax returns equivalent Markdown even when formatting normalizes.\\n3. **Touched-region normalization:** editing one region does not rewrite unrelated source.\\n\\nEach extension declares its preservation behavior. Source spans, syntax metadata, raw slices, opaque nodes, and diagnostics must support these guarantees.\\n\\n### One semantic engine, multiple bindings\\n\\nCore commands, transactions, selection semantics, validation, and extension behavior live outside framework packages. React, Svelte, and Solid adapters bind lifecycle and rendering only. A semantic trace suite runs against every adapter.\\n\\n### Progressive capability\\n\\nConsumers should be able to choose:\\n\\n* Headless parsing and transformation\\n* Read-only rendering\\n* Source editing\\n* Visual editing\\n* Split editing and preview\\n* Composable editor chrome\\n* Assets and embeds\\n* Persistence\\n* Collaboration and editorial workflows\\n\\nOptional layers must not inflate or constrain the core.\\n\\n### Secure defaults\\n\\nRaw HTML is disabled by default. Links, pasted HTML, custom embeds, assets, and server operations use explicit policies. Parsing and rendering must defend against unsafe URLs, script injection, malformed input, and resource-exhaustion attacks.\\n\\n## Product surface\\n\\n### Foundational editing\\n\\n* Paragraphs, headings, block quotes, thematic breaks\\n* Bold, italic, strike, inline code, links\\n* Ordered, unordered, nested, and task lists\\n* Code blocks with language metadata\\n* Tables and alignment\\n* Images, files, and embeds\\n* Selection-aware commands and keyboard shortcuts\\n* Undo/redo and transaction history\\n* Drag/drop and clipboard import\\n* Placeholder, read-only, and disabled states\\n* Markdown, HTML, JSON, and plain-text import/export\\n\\n### Markdown-native authoring\\n\\n* CommonMark baseline and GFM default\\n* Frontmatter\\n* Footnotes\\n* Math\\n* Callouts and directives\\n* Diagram and custom fenced blocks\\n* Reference links and definitions\\n* Custom components through an opt-in portable directive contract\\n* Formatting and diagnostics\\n* Source, visual, split, preview, and read-only modes\\n* Source-preservation reporting\\n\\nExecutable mixed-framework syntax should remain a separate opt-in extension family because it weakens portability, security, and renderer independence.\\n\\n### Editor UI\\n\\n* Fixed and floating toolbars\\n* Bubble controls\\n* Slash-command palette\\n* Link editor\\n* Table controls\\n* File and embed dialogs\\n* Outline and heading navigation\\n* Diagnostics panel\\n* Comments and suggestion surfaces\\n* Version/history interface\\n* Responsive and mobile toolbar patterns\\n\\nUIFn should own visual components, focus management, overlays, themes, and accessibility primitives. mdfn should own editor state and semantic actions.\\n\\n### Collaboration and editorial workflows\\n\\n* Realtime document synchronization\\n* Offline edits and reconnect\\n* Presence and selections\\n* Threaded comments\\n* Suggestions and review state\\n* Version history\\n* Schema-aware migration\\n* Authorization and audit hooks\\n\\nThese capabilities should use a proven collaborative data engine behind mdfn-owned interfaces. The project should not invent merge algorithms from first principles.\\n\\n## Ecosystem fit\\n\\n### UIFn\\n\\nUse UIFn for editor chrome and accessibility primitives. Reuse its package layering:\\n\\ncore → DOM → adapter kit → framework adapters → components/style → registry\\n\\nmdfn must add stronger semantic parity tests so framework packages cannot fork editing behavior.\\n\\n### DocsFn\\n\\nDocsFn should consume the canonical mdfn Markdown pipeline through a docs-specific extension set and a compatibility projection to its rendering model. Existing behavior should migrate incrementally, not through a flag day.\\n\\n### ContentFn\\n\\nAdd an explicit Markdown content value rather than silently reinterpreting rich-text blocks:\\n\\n```ts\\ntype MarkdownContent = {\\n  kind: \\\"markdown\\\";\\n  source: string;\\n  dialect: \\\"commonmark\\\" | \\\"gfm\\\";\\n  extensions: string[];\\n  allowRawHtml: boolean;\\n  schemaVersion: number;\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### CmsFn\\n\\nCmsFn authoring can compose mdfn framework components, while stored content remains framework-independent. Existing rich-text data needs explicit migration and compatibility rules.\\n\\n### FileFn\\n\\nIntegrate through an asset-provider interface. Markdown stores durable asset identifiers or stable references with fallback URLs and metadata; upload orchestration remains in FileFn.\\n\\n### SearchFn\\n\\nExpose deterministic helpers for plain text, headings, links, and chunks so search indexing derives from the same document interpretation used by editing and rendering.\\n\\n### AuthFn, HTTP, and database packages\\n\\nOptional mdfn server packages should accept shared HTTP and database contracts. Any incoming database adapter must be wrapped internally with mdfn's schema before use. Authentication and authorization should integrate through hooks and middleware rather than hard-coded providers.\\n\\n## Source and persistence contract\\n\\nA persisted snapshot can follow this shape:\\n\\n```ts\\ninterface MdfnSnapshot {\\n  format: \\\"mdfn\\\";\\n  version: 1;\\n  markdown: string;\\n  document?: MdfnJSON;\\n  schemaHash: string;\\n  diagnostics: Diagnostic[];\\n  sidecar?: {\\n    comments?: CommentThread[];\\n    suggestions?: Suggestion[];\\n    assetMetadata?: AssetMetadata[];\\n  };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe structured document cache is valid only when its schema hash and source identity match. Comments and suggestions live in a sidecar because they are editorial state, not portable Markdown syntax.\\n\\n## Differentiation\\n\\nmdfn's durable advantage is the combination of:\\n\\n* Markdown-first source guarantees\\n* Framework-neutral semantics\\n* Equal multi-framework adapters\\n* Self-hostable optional services\\n* Extensible syntax and node behavior\\n* Explicit compatibility with the rest of Superfunctions\\n* Inspectable security and migration contracts\\n* A path from headless parsing to collaborative authoring without replacing the document model\\n\\n## Success measures\\n\\nProduct success should be measured through verifiable outcomes:\\n\\n* No-edit and supported-syntax round-trip corpus pass rates\\n* Unknown-syntax preservation rate\\n* Cross-framework semantic trace parity\\n* Keystroke and parse latency budgets\\n* Browser, mobile, IME, clipboard, SSR, and accessibility conformance\\n* Security corpus and fuzz-test results\\n* Packed-consumer success across supported module formats\\n* Successful DocsFn compatibility projection\\n* Successful ContentFn and CmsFn Markdown-type adoption\\n* FileFn asset and SearchFn extraction interoperability\\n* Number of downstream products using the shared engine without forks\\n\\n## Scope controls\\n\\nThe first stable foundation should prioritize:\\n\\n* Content and preservation contracts\\n* Headless transactions, commands, extensions, parsing, serialization, rendering, and diagnostics\\n* Browser editing primitives and source mode\\n* Vanilla DOM plus framework parity\\n* Essential editor UI\\n* Security, packaging, performance, and migration gates\\n\\nCollaboration, comments, suggestions, history, AI, and full server persistence should remain optional packages and may advance after the foundation proves the document and extension contracts.\\n\\n## Key product risks\\n\\n* Choosing preservation semantics after coding begins\\n* Allowing runtime JSON to become the undocumented canonical format\\n* Adapter-specific command or selection logic\\n* Excessive stable package count\\n* Silent loss of unsupported syntax\\n* Unsafe HTML, links, embeds, or pasted content\\n* Treating browser, mobile, IME, clipboard, and SSR behavior as late polish\\n* Coupling optional collaboration or persistence into the core\\n* Forcing ecosystem adoption before compatibility projections and migrations exist\\n\\n## Open product decisions\\n\\n* Mandatory preservation level for the first stable release\\n* Default extension set beyond CommonMark and GFM\\n* Whether frontmatter, footnotes, math, directives, and diagrams are default or opt-in\\n* First-release boundary between browser foundation and collaboration\\n* Stable public facade versus advanced direct imports\\n* Initial performance and bundle budgets\\n* Versioning policy for syntax, schema, snapshots, and sidecars\\n* Migration requirements for existing content stores\\n* Whether source maps and diagnostics are exposed as a stable public API\\n\\n## Rough effort model\\n\\nThese are engineering-effort bands for backlog shaping, not delivery dates:\\n\\n* Architecture contracts and risk spikes: **4–6 engineer-weeks**\\n* Integrated prototype: **8–12 engineer-weeks**\\n* Production headless and browser foundation: **9–15 engineer-months**\\n* Complete self-hosted authoring platform: **18–30 engineer-months**\\n\\nThe production scope is best handled by a small cross-functional team with deep editing/browser expertise, framework integration experience, and dedicated security and accessibility review.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nProceed through contract-first risk reduction. Approve the product thesis, preservation guarantees, package boundaries, extension contract, and ecosystem compatibility strategy before selecting final implementation dependencies or building broad UI. The first implementation milestone should prove round trips, unknown syntax, transactions, source mapping, and equal vanilla/framework behavior on a representative syntax corpus.\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/ideation-and-product-rationale-sfn-20-build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-b7127afd8102\",\"slugId\":\"b7127afd8102\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:09:28.718Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:09:29.967Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SFN-20\",\"title\":\"Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform\"}}"
      }
