        "text": "{\"id\":\"SFN-20\",\"title\":\"Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform\",\"description\":\"## Executive summary\\n\\nCreate **mdfn**, a framework-neutral Markdown authoring platform for the Superfunctions ecosystem. The product should combine a headless editing engine, first-class Markdown parsing and serialization, a vanilla DOM surface, equal framework adapters, composable editor UI built with UIFn, and optional self-hosted collaboration and platform integrations.\\n\\nThe durable contract is Markdown source. Runtime editor state may use a structured document model, but import, editing, export, migrations, diagnostics, and extension behavior must preserve source meaning and avoid silently discarding unsupported content.\\n\\n## Problem / opportunity\\n\\nSuperfunctions has useful pieces for Markdown rendering, portable content blocks, file handling, search, UI, authentication, HTTP, and database access, but it does not yet have a shared authoring substrate. Existing Markdown compilation is renderer-oriented, and existing rich-text authoring surfaces do not provide a complete editing engine.\\n\\nWithout mdfn, each product must independently solve schema design, selection behavior, commands, history, clipboard handling, source mode, framework integration, UI parity, persistence, collaboration, and security. A shared Markdown-native platform can remove that duplication while improving interoperability across DocsFn, ContentFn, CmsFn, FileFn, SearchFn, AuthFn, and the shared HTTP/database packages.\\n\\n## Expected outcome\\n\\nA production-ready, self-hostable Markdown platform that supports:\\n\\n* Headless document and command APIs independent of UI framework\\n* Canonical Markdown parsing, serialization, formatting, and diagnostics\\n* Visual, source, split, preview, and read-only modes\\n* Vanilla DOM integration plus semantically equivalent React, Svelte, and Solid adapters\\n* UIFn-based editor chrome without coupling editing semantics to UIFn\\n* Extensible schema, syntax, commands, keymaps, paste/input rules, rendering, migrations, and tests\\n* Optional collaboration, presence, comments, suggestions, history, assets, persistence, and AI integration\\n* Reuse by DocsFn, ContentFn, CmsFn, FileFn, and SearchFn through explicit compatibility contracts\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n* Define the source-preservation contract before implementation.\\n* Support byte-identical no-edit import/export, semantic round trips for supported syntax, and touched-region normalization.\\n* Establish CommonMark as the baseline and GFM as the default dialect.\\n* Preserve unsupported constructs as diagnosable opaque nodes rather than silently dropping them.\\n* Provide a small stable package graph with explicit dependency directions.\\n* Keep the core framework-neutral, UI-neutral, storage-neutral, transport-neutral, and collaboration-neutral.\\n* Provide equal behavior across vanilla DOM, React, Svelte, and Solid.\\n* Use shared Superfunctions abstractions for HTTP, database, authentication, files, search, and UI.\\n* Ship conformance, parity, security, accessibility, browser, IME, clipboard, SSR, performance, migration, and packed-consumer test gates.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n\\n* Building browser editing primitives, history, clipboard semantics, or collaborative merge algorithms from scratch.\\n* Coupling the core document model to a UI framework or component library.\\n* Replacing DocsFn, ContentFn, CmsFn, FileFn, SearchFn, AuthFn, or shared packages.\\n* Making executable mixed-framework syntax part of the default portable Markdown dialect.\\n* Enabling raw HTML by default.\\n* Creating one workspace package per document node or mark.\\n* Shipping implementation as part of this backlog-capture item.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n* A reviewed product and architecture contract defines the durable Markdown model, runtime document model, source spans, syntax metadata, opaque-node behavior, diagnostics, migrations, and versioning.\\n* The package dependency graph and public/private package boundaries are explicit and mechanically enforceable.\\n* The extension SDK contract covers schema, parsing, serialization, HTML rendering and sanitization, commands, shortcuts, input and paste rules, plugins, migrations, DOM renderers, and conformance tests.\\n* A framework contract defines vanilla DOM behavior and adapter parity requirements for React, Svelte, and Solid.\\n* An editor capability matrix covers foundational editing, Markdown-native syntax, assets and embeds, UI surfaces, collaboration, persistence, import/export, and read-only rendering.\\n* Ecosystem integration contracts are defined for UIFn, DocsFn, ContentFn, CmsFn, FileFn, SearchFn, AuthFn, and shared HTTP/database packages.\\n* Security defaults and threat models cover raw HTML, links, pasted HTML, custom embeds, asset access, collaboration authorization, and denial-of-service inputs.\\n* Release gates cover parser and serializer conformance, round trips, unknown syntax, framework parity, browsers, mobile, IME, clipboard, SSR, accessibility, security, fuzzing, performance, packaging, migrations, and ecosystem adoption.\\n* The work is decomposed into independently verifiable delivery phases with explicit exit criteria.\\n* The two associated Linear documents remain the canonical rationale and delivery-plan references for this issue.\\n\\n## Dependencies / risks\\n\\n* The source-preservation guarantee constrains parser, runtime model, serializer, migrations, and collaboration design; postponing it risks irreversible architecture mistakes.\\n* A structured editing kernel and collaboration engine must be selected through focused risk spikes and license, bundle, SSR, browser, mobile, and IME evaluation.\\n* Framework adapters can drift unless all behavior is implemented in shared core and adapter layers and verified through the same semantic trace suite.\\n* UIFn package-boundary enforcement must be reliable before mdfn adopts the same graph-verification pattern.\\n* DocsFn adoption requires a compatibility projection so existing rendering behavior can migrate without a flag day.\\n* ContentFn and CmsFn need explicit Markdown content types and migration paths rather than silent reinterpretation of current rich-text data.\\n* Collaboration, comments, and suggestions require stable identifiers, schema-aware migrations, authorization hooks, auditability, and offline conflict handling.\\n* Untrusted Markdown, HTML, links, embeds, assets, and pasted content create security and resource-exhaustion risks.\\n* Package surface area can grow rapidly; stable packages must remain few, with optional capabilities and subpath exports used deliberately.\\n\\n## Open questions\\n\\n* Which source-preservation level is mandatory for the first stable release, and which syntax classes may normalize?\\n* Which structured editing kernel best satisfies the framework, browser, SSR, IME, bundle, licensing, and plugin requirements?\\n* Which syntax belongs in the default dialect versus opt-in extension packs?\\n* What is the stable public boundary between mdfn parsing/rendering and DocsFn-specific behavior?\\n* Should collaboration and persistence ship in the first stable platform release or follow the headless/browser foundation?\\n* Which content migrations are required for existing ContentFn and CmsFn data?\\n* What are the initial performance budgets for document size, parse latency, keystroke latency, memory, and collaboration fan-out?\\n* Which capabilities belong in the facade package versus explicit advanced imports?\\n\\n## Associated documents\\n\\n* [Ideation & Product Rationale — SFN-20: Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform](<https://linear.app/21n/document/ideation-and-product-rationale-sfn-20-build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-b7127afd8102>)\\n* [Architecture & Delivery Plan — SFN-20: Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform](<https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-sfn-20-build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-5398a216a19b>)\",\"priority\":{\"value\":0,\"name\":\"No priority\"},\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-20/build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-markdown-authoring-platform\",\"gitBranchName\":\"aravink/sfn-20-build-mdfn-as-the-superfunctions-markdown-authoring-platform\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:06:54.445Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-12T15:50:06.708Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"completedAt\":null,\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-12T15:50:06.695Z\",\"canceledAt\":null,\"dueDate\":null,\"slaStartedAt\":null,\"slaMediumRiskAt\":null,\"slaHighRiskAt\":null,\"slaBreachesAt\":null,\"status\":\"In Progress\",\"statusType\":\"started\",\"labels\":[],\"attachments\":[],\"documents\":[{\"id\":\"e0607ce5-7d2f-48bd-ab0b-0242c445dfc7\",\"title\":\"Architecture & Delivery Plan — SFN-20: Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform\"},{\"id\":\"6e4b07aa-23dc-4146-a987-093fdbfa9da2\",\"title\":\"Ideation & Product Rationale — SFN-20: Build mdfn as the Superfunctions Markdown authoring platform\"}],\"stateHistory\":[{\"state\":{\"id\":\"85023df2-b48b-42ee-b4b4-fe32f00e6c23\",\"name\":\"Backlog\",\"type\":\"backlog\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:06:54.445Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:15:26.749Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"49bc7d5e-06b3-4267-be83-bd9f2f0c42ec\",\"name\":\"Todo\",\"type\":\"unstarted\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-12T13:15:26.749Z\",\"endedAt\":\"2026-08-12T15:50:06.704Z\"},{\"state\":{\"id\":\"6cbd5c48-6254-4e4e-b642-514e70683dc1\",\"name\":\"In Progress\",\"type\":\"started\"},\"startedAt\":\"2026-08-12T15:50:06.704Z\",\"endedAt\":null}],\"releases\":[],\"createdBy\":\"Aravind\",\"createdById\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"assignee\":\"Aravind\",\"assigneeId\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"project\":\"Super functions t1\",\"projectId\":\"ddb7a6a8-0c46-48f2-80c1-e4533bbe1750\",\"team\":\"SF Next\",\"teamId\":\"8ce97d01-cdf7-47e4-a213-c3dbddc86d63\",\"cycleId\"